    {
      "to": "Priya Nair <priya.nair@devfleet.io>",
      "subject": "Re: Quick question | byLLM structured output",
      "message": "Hi Priya,\n\nShort answer: yes \u2014 byLLM supports typed/structured outputs natively, so you won't need to parse plain text yourself.\n\nIn Jac, you declare a function with a typed return value and delegate the implementation to the LLM using the `by llm()` syntax. The runtime enforces that the model's output conforms to the declared type, so you get structured data directly.\n\nFor your log triage case, you could define a struct with the fields you need and have the LLM return it:\n\n```\nstruct LogEntry {\n    severity: string;\n    component: string;\n    message: string;\n}\n\nfn parse_log_line(line: string): LogEntry by llm();\n```\n\nGiven input like `ERROR | walker_spawn | context missing`, the model returns a `LogEntry` object with the fields populated. You then access them directly (e.g., `entry.severity`, `entry.component`, `entry.message`) \u2014 no manual text parsing required.\n\nThe runtime validates the model output against the declared type, which gives you a safety net if the model returns something unexpected.\n\nHappy to help with any follow-ups as you evaluate.\n\nBest,\nJaseci Team"
    }
  ],
